<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tunisia</h1><p class="st-meta">1957 CE – present · capital Tunis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A North African republic, independent from France since 1956, where the 2011 revolution began the Arab Spring and where Kais Saied has held power since 2019.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tunisia">Kingdom of Tunisia</a>, a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> since 1881, became independent on 20 March 1956, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Habib Bourguiba</a> as prime minister. A year later Tunisia was declared a republic, and Bourguiba became its first president. The nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-destour">Neo-Destour</a> and the labour organisation UGTT had been key parts of the struggle for independence.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From independence until 2011 the government and the Constitutional Democratic Rally, formerly Neo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/destour">Destour</a>, were effectively one. In November 1987 doctors declared Bourguiba unfit to rule, and Zine El Abidine Ben Ali assumed the presidency in a bloodless coup. Ben Ali was re-elected every five years until protests following the death of Mohamed Bouazizi on 4 January 2011 led him to flee on 14 January 2011. Amnesty International and Freedom House documented that basic rights were not respected under his rule. The Ennahda Movement won the 2011 Constituent Assembly election, and a constitution was adopted on 26 January 2014.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Kais Saied became president in October 2019. On 25 July 2021 he suspended parliament and dismissed the prime minister, and a new constitution was approved in a referendum in 2022 on a turnout of 30%. Rached Ghannouchi and other opponents were arrested in 2023, and Saied won a second term on 6 October 2024 with more than 90% of the vote on a turnout of 28.8%.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tunisia.jpg" alt="Tunisia" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Various Wikimedia Commons' contributors · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tunisia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1957 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tunis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Habib Bourguiba, Zine El Abidine Ben Ali, Moncef Marzouki, Beji Caid Essebsi, Kais Saied</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (state religion), small Christian and Jewish communities</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic (official), French widely used</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Tunisia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1992&amp;focus=tunisia" aria-label="Tunisia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Tunisia lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5693 4389L5693 4389"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5693 4389h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5693 4389h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5693 4389h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tunis. <a href="/atlas?year=1992&amp;focus=tunisia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
